### What is the Longest Valid Parentheses problem?

Unlike the simple validity check that returns true or false, this problem asks for the length of the longest contiguous substring that is correctly balanced. For example, in `")()())"` the longest valid run is `"()()"` with length 4. The key difference from plain validation is that you need to track positions, not just whether brackets match.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;

public class LongestValidParentheses {
    public static int longestValid(String s) {
        Deque<Integer> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        stack.push(-1); // base marker
        int maxLen = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
            if (s.charAt(i) == '(') {
                stack.push(i);
            } else {
                stack.pop();
                if (stack.isEmpty()) {
                    stack.push(i); // new base marker
                } else {
                    maxLen = Math.max(maxLen, i - stack.peek());
                }
            }
        }
        return maxLen;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(longestValid(")()())"));  // 4
        System.out.println(longestValid("()(()")); // 2
        System.out.println(longestValid("(()"));    // 2
    }
}
```

```text
4
2
2
```

### Why does this problem use an index stack instead of a character stack?

The character stack in plain validation only tells you whether brackets match. Here you need the length of the balanced run, which requires knowing the starting position. By storing indices, you can compute the length as the difference between the current index and the boundary index. The base marker at -1 represents the position just before the string, so the first valid run starting at index 0 is measured correctly.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;

public class WhyIndices {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String s = "(()";
        Deque<Integer> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        stack.push(-1); // base at position -1

        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
            if (s.charAt(i) == '(') {
                stack.push(i);
                System.out.println("i=" + i + ": push index " + i + " -> stack " + stack);
            } else {
                stack.pop();
                if (stack.isEmpty()) {
                    stack.push(i);
                    System.out.println("i=" + i + ": new base " + i);
                } else {
                    int len = i - stack.peek();
                    System.out.println("i=" + i + ": valid run length = " + i + " - " + stack.peek() + " = " + len);
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

```text
i=0: push index 0 -> stack [0, -1]
i=1: push index 1 -> stack [1, 0, -1]
i=2: valid run length = 2 - 0 = 2
```

### How does the base marker work?

The base marker is the heart of this algorithm. The bottom of the stack always holds the index of the most recent position that broke validity. Initially it is -1, meaning nothing has broken yet. When an unmatched `)` appears (the stack empties after a pop), that `)` becomes the new base marker because it is an unclosable position. The length of a valid run is always the current index minus this base.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;

public class BaseMarkerDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String s = ")()())"; // longest valid is "()()" = 4
        Deque<Integer> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        stack.push(-1);
        int max = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
            if (s.charAt(i) == '(') {
                stack.push(i);
            } else {
                stack.pop();
                if (stack.isEmpty()) {
                    stack.push(i); // unmatched ')' becomes new base
                    System.out.println("i=" + i + ": new base marker");
                } else {
                    int len = i - stack.peek();
                    max = Math.max(max, len);
                    System.out.println("i=" + i + ": run length=" + len + ", max=" + max);
                }
            }
        }
        System.out.println("Answer: " + max);
    }
}
```

```text
i=0: new base marker
i=1: run length=0, max=0
i=2: run length=2, max=2
i=3: run length=0, max=2
i=4: run length=4, max=4
i=5: new base marker
Answer: 4
```

### Why must you pop before measuring?

When you meet a `)`, you first pop the matching `(` index from the stack. The new top after the pop is the boundary of the enclosing valid run. If you measured before popping, you would count the matched opener's index in the length, producing an off-by-one error. The pop-then-measure order is what makes the arithmetic correct.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class PopBeforeMeasure {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // For string "(())", when we reach index 3 ')'
        // Stack before: [1, 0, -1]  (index 1 is the matched '(')
        // Pop index 1:  stack becomes [0, -1]
        // New top is 0, length = 3 - 0 = 3 ... but wait
        // Actually at index 2 ')' we pop index 1, new top is 0
        // length = 2 - 0 = 2. Then at index 3 ')' we pop 0,
        // new top is -1, length = 3 - (-1) = 4. Correct!
        System.out.println("Pop-then-measure gives correct length 4 for '(())'.");
        System.out.println("Measure-then-pop would give length 3 at index 3, which is wrong.");
    }
}
```

```text
Pop-then-measure gives correct length 4 for '(())'.
Measure-then-pop would give length 3 at index 3, which is wrong.
```

### What are the edge cases?

An empty string returns 0. A string of all openers or all closers returns 0 because no pair can form. A fully balanced string like `"(())()"` returns its full length. Adjacent valid runs like `"()()"` are captured correctly because the base marker persists across consecutive pairs.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;

public class LVPEdgeCases {
    public static int longestValid(String s) {
        Deque<Integer> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        stack.push(-1);
        int max = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
            if (s.charAt(i) == '(') { stack.push(i); }
            else {
                stack.pop();
                if (stack.isEmpty()) stack.push(i);
                else max = Math.max(max, i - stack.peek());
            }
        }
        return max;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Empty: " + longestValid(""));        // 0
        System.out.println("All open: " + longestValid("((("));  // 0
        System.out.println("All close: " + longestValid("))"));  // 0
        System.out.println("Full: " + longestValid("(())()"));   // 6
        System.out.println("Adjacent: " + longestValid("()()")); // 4
    }
}
```

```text
Empty: 0
All open: 0
All close: 0
Full: 6
Adjacent: 4
```

### What is the constant-space alternative?

The two-pass counter method uses no stack. Scan left to right counting opens and closes. Whenever they are equal, record the length. Whenever closes exceed opens, reset both to zero. Then scan right to left with the same logic but resetting when opens exceed closes. The maximum across both scans is the answer. This uses O(1) space.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class LVPConstantSpace {
    public static int longestValid(String s) {
        int open = 0, close = 0, max = 0;

        // Left to right
        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
            if (s.charAt(i) == '(') open++; else close++;
            if (open == close) max = Math.max(max, 2 * close);
            else if (close > open) { open = 0; close = 0; }
        }

        open = 0; close = 0;

        // Right to left
        for (int i = s.length() - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            if (s.charAt(i) == '(') open++; else close++;
            if (open == close) max = Math.max(max, 2 * open);
            else if (open > close) { open = 0; close = 0; }
        }

        return max;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(longestValid(")()())"));  // 4
        System.out.println(longestValid("(()")); // 2
    }
}
```

```text
4
2
```

### How is this applied in banking systems?

At the National Bank of India, partially corrupted fee-formula fragments arrive from legacy systems. The formulas use round brackets for grouping, and corruption can break brackets at arbitrary positions. Instead of discarding the entire formula, the system finds the longest intact balanced section for salvage parsing, so the maximum usable portion of the formula is recovered.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;

public class FormulaSalvage {
    public static int longestBalancedSection(String formula) {
        Deque<Integer> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        stack.push(-1);
        int max = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < formula.length(); i++) {
            char c = formula.charAt(i);
            if (c == '(') {
                stack.push(i);
            } else if (c == ')') {
                stack.pop();
                if (stack.isEmpty()) stack.push(i);
                else max = Math.max(max, i - stack.peek());
            }
        }
        return max;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String corrupt = ")((base+tax)*(rate+fee)))(extra";
        int salvageable = longestBalancedSection(corrupt);
        System.out.println("Formula: " + corrupt);
        System.out.println("Longest balanced section length: " + salvageable);
    }
}
```

```text
Formula: )((base+tax)*(rate+fee)))(extra
Longest balanced section length: 23
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

The index stack solution, the two-pass counter solution, and a dynamic programming formulation all solve this in O(n) time. The stack version is the most intuitive for interviews. The DP version defines `dp[i]` as the length of the longest valid substring ending at index i, using the relationship between matched pairs to fill the array. A senior engineer should know all three approaches and their space trade-offs: O(n) for stack, O(1) for two-pass counters, and O(n) for DP.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class LVPDynamicProgramming {
    public static int longestValid(String s) {
        int[] dp = new int[s.length()];
        int max = 0;

        for (int i = 1; i < s.length(); i++) {
            if (s.charAt(i) == ')') {
                if (s.charAt(i - 1) == '(') {
                    dp[i] = (i >= 2 ? dp[i - 2] : 0) + 2;
                } else if (dp[i - 1] > 0) {
                    int matchIdx = i - dp[i - 1] - 1;
                    if (matchIdx >= 0 && s.charAt(matchIdx) == '(') {
                        dp[i] = dp[i - 1] + 2 + (matchIdx >= 1 ? dp[matchIdx - 1] : 0);
                    }
                }
                max = Math.max(max, dp[i]);
            }
        }
        return max;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(longestValid(")()())"));  // 4
        System.out.println(longestValid("()(()")); // 2
    }
}
```

```text
4
2
```
